idemics using search engine query data. Nature 457(7232):1012&#x2013;1014. https://doi.org/10.1038/nature07634</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/nature07634</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Gluskin RT, Johansson MA, Santillana M, Brownstein JS (2014) Evaluation of internet-based dengue query data: Google Dengue Trends. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 8(2):e2713. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pntd.0002713</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1371/journal.pntd.0002713</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Guo P et al (2017) Developing a dengue forecast model using machine learning: a case study in China. PLoS Negl Trop Dis 11(10):e0005973. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pntd.0005973</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1371/journal.pntd.0005973</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hettiarachchige C, von Cavallar S, Lynar T, Hickson RI, Gambhir M (2018) Risk prediction system for dengue transmission based on high resolution weather data. PLoS One 13(12):e0208203. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0208203</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0208203</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hilbe JM (2011) Negative binomial regression. Cambridge University Press</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1017/CBO9780511973420</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ho HT et al (2018) Using Google Trends to examine the spatio-temporal incidence and behavioral patterns of dengue disease: a case study in metropolitan Manila, Philippines. Trop Med Infect Dis 3(4):118</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Husnayain A, Anis F, Lazuardi L (2019) Correlation between Google Trends on dengue fever and national surveillance report in Indonesia. Glob Health Action 12(1):1552652. https://doi.org/10.1080/16549716.2018.1552652</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1080/16