h artificial intelligence (AI) support. Digit Health. 2024 Dec 8;10:20552076241295565. doi: 10.1177/20552076241295565. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1177/20552076241295565</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC11626650</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">39655059</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wang L, Lyu C, Ji T, et al. Document-level machine translation with large language models. arXiv. 2023 Apr 5; doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2304.02210. Preprint posted online on. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.48550/arXiv.2304.02210</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kocmi T, Federmann C. Large language models are state-of-the-art evaluators of translation quality. arXiv. 2023 Feb 28; doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2302.14520. Preprint posted online on. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.48550/arXiv.2302.14520</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Wassie AK, Molaei M, Moslem Y. Domain-specific translation with open-source large language models: resource-oriented analysis. arXiv. 2024 Dec 8; doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2412.05862. Preprint posted online on. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.48550/arXiv.2412.05862</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Rios M. Instruction-tuned large language models for machine translation in the medical domain. arXiv. 2024 Aug 29; doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2408.16440. Preprint posted online on. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.48550/arXiv.2408.16440</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Cardey S, Greenfield P, Wu X. Designing a controlled language for the machine translation of medical protocols: the case of English to Chinese. 6th Conference of the Association for Machine Translation in the Americas; Sep 28 to Oct 2, 2004; Washington, DC. [27-02-2026]. Presented at. URL. Accessed.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1007/978-3-540-30194-3_5</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Cultural diversity of Australia. Australian Bureau of Statistics. 2022.  [27-02-2026]. https://www.abs.gov.au/articles/cultural-diversity-australia URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chen X, Zhou W, Hoda R, Li A, Bain C, Poon P. Exploring the opportunities of large language models for summarizing palliative care consultations: a pilot comparative study. Digit Health. 2024 Nov 20;10:20552076241293932. doi: 10.1177/20552076241293932. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1177/20552076241293932</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC11577459</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">39569395</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Papineni K, Roukos S, Ward T, Zhu WJ. BLEU: a method for automatic evaluation of machine translation. ACL &#x2019;02: Proceedings of the 40th Annual Meeting on Association for Computational Linguistics; Jul 7-12, 2002; Philadelphia, PA. Presented at. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3115/1073083.1073135</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Popovi&#x107; M. chrF: character n-gram F-score for automatic MT evaluation. Proceedings of the Tenth Workshop on Statistical Machine Translation; Sep 17-18, 2017; Lisbon, Portugal. Presented at. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.18653/v1/W15-3049</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Banerjee S, Lavie A. METEOR: an automatic metric for MT evaluation with improved correlation with human judgments. ACL Workshop on Intrinsic and Extrinsic Evaluation Measures for Machine Translation and/or Summarization; Jun 29, 2005; Ann Arbor, MI. [28-02-2026]. Presented at. URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Baek Y, Lee K, Ki D, Park C, Lee HG, Choo J. Towards accurate translation via semantically appropriate application of lexical constraints. arXiv. 2023 Jun 21; doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2306.12089. Preprint posted online on. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.48550/arXiv.2306.12089</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Du&#x161;ek O, Haji&#x10d; J, Hlav&#xe1;&#x10d;ov&#xe1; J, et al. Machine translation of medical texts in the Khresmoi project. Proceedings of the Ninth Workshop on Statistical Machine Translation; Jun 26-27, 2014; Baltimore, MD. [28-02-2026]. Presented at. URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Elshin D, Karpachev N, Gruzdev B, et al. From general LLM to translation: how we dramatically improve translation quality using human evaluation data for LLM finetuning. Proceedings of the Ninth Conference on Machine Translation; Nov 15-16, 2024; Miami, FL. Presented at. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.18653/v1/2024.wmt-1.17</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Vieira LN, O&#x2019;Hagan M, O&#x2019;Sullivan C. Understanding the societal impacts of machine translation: a critical review of the literature on medical and legal use cases. Inf Commun Soc. 2021;24(11):1515&#x2013;1532. doi: 10.1080/1369118X.2020.1776370. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1080/1369118X.2020.1776370</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ismayilli TM. Navigating complexities in medical text translation: challenges, strategies, and solutions. Acta Glob Humanit Linguar. 2024;1(2):170&#x2013;176. doi: 10.69760/aghel.01024080. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.69760/aghel.01024080</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zhang B, Haddow B, Birch A. Prompting large language model for machine translation: a case study. ICML&#x2019;23: Proceedings of the 40th International Conference on Machine Learning; Jul 23-29, 2023; Honolulu, HI. Presented at. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.5555/3618408.3620130</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Garcia X, Bansal Y, Cherry C, et al. The unreasonable effectiveness of few-shot learning for machine translation. ICML&#x2019;23: Proceedings of the 40th International Conference on Machine Learning; Jul 23-29, 2023; Honolulu, HI. Presented at. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.5555/3618408.3618846</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Eshbo&#x2019;riyevich AY. Problems of translation of medical terms.  [28-02-2026];International Conference on Linguistics, Literature and Translation (London) 2024 2:1&#x2013;5. https://top-conferences.us/index.php/ICLLT/article/view/226 URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Croxford E, Gao Y, First E, et al. Evaluating clinical AI summaries with large language models as judges. NPJ Digit Med. 2025 Nov 5;8(1):640. doi: 10.1038/s41746-025-02005-2. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41746-025-02005-2</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12589481</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41193667</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Abbasian M, Khatibi E, Azimi I, et al. Foundation metrics for evaluating effectiveness of healthcare conversations powered by generative AI. NPJ Digit Med. 2024 Mar 29;7(1):82. doi: 10.1038/s41746-024-01074-z. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41746-024-01074-z</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC10980701</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">38553625</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Asgari E, Monta&#xf1;a-Brown N, Dubois M, et al. A framework to assess clinical safety and hallucination rates of LLMs for medical text summarisation. NPJ Digit Med. 2025 May 13;8(1):274. doi: 10.1038/s41746-025-01670-7. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/s41746-025-01670-7</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12075489</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">40360677</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41969604</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>04</Month><Day>13</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>04</Month><Day>13</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2405-5263</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>12</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Mar</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Osteoporosis and sarcopenia</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Osteoporos Sarcopenia</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Artificial intelligence-assisted screening reveals high prevalence of osteoporosis in Vietnamese adults using pelvic and hip radiographs.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>18</StartPage><EndPage>25</EndPage><MedlinePgn>18-25</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.afos.2026.01.002</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Osteoporosis is a silent disease with low screening rates in many developing countries. This study aimed to evaluate the feasibility of using an artificial intelligence (AI)-based system to screen osteoporosis from pelvic and hip radiographs in Vietnam.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We conducted a cross-sectional study at a tertiary medical center in Central Vietnam in 2023. A total of 2000 consecutive pelvic and hip radiographs from patients aged &#x2265; 40 years were collected. After excluding poor-quality images, 1987 radiographs were analyzed using an AI-based software designed to estimate bone mineral density (BMD) from plain radiographs and derive T-scores. Osteoporosis was defined as a T-score &#x2264; -2.5. Patient characteristics, radiographic findings, and risk factors for osteoporosis were analyzed.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Among 1987 patients (mean age 66.4&#xa0;&#xb1;&#xa0;15.1 years; 41.3% men), osteoporosis was identified in 872 patients (43.9%). The prevalence increased with age and was higher in women than in men (58.7% vs 22.8%, P&#xa0;&lt;&#xa0;0.001). Osteoporosis was associated with femoral neck (OR&#xa0;=&#xa0;3.8, 95% CI: 2.7-5.2) and intertrochanteric fractures (OR&#xa0;=&#xa0;7.0, 95% CI: 4.5-11.0). Patients with lower T-scores had a higher risk of hip fractures, especially those with T-scores &#x2264; -3.0 (OR&#xa0;=&#xa0;11.5, 95% CI: 5.5-24.5).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">AI-based analysis of pelvic and hip radiographs is a feasible and effective tool for osteoporosis screening in Vietnam. The prevalence of osteoporosis in this hospital-based setting was high, particularly among elderly women. AI-assisted screening may offer an accessible strategy for early detection of osteoporosis in resource-limited settings.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2026 The Korean Society of Osteoporosis. Publishing services by Elsevier B.V.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Dat Minh</ForeName><Initials>DM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Orthopaedic and Plastic Center, Hue Central Hospital, Hue City, Viet Nam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wu</LastName><ForeName>Chih-Hsing</ForeName><Initials>CH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Family Medicine, National Cheng Kung University Hospital, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gerontology, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Tuan Van</ForeName><Initials>TV</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>T&#xe2;m Anh Research Institute, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Population Health, UNSW Medicine, UNSW Sydney, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre for Health Technologies, University of Technology Sydney (UTS), Sydney, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ho-Pham</LastName><ForeName>Lan T</ForeName><Initials>LT</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>BioMedical Research Center, Pham Ngoc Thach University of Medicine, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Clinical Genetics Research Group, Saigon Precision Medicine Research Center, Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dang</LastName><ForeName>Kim Thi Hoang</ForeName><Initials>KTH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Diagnostic Imaging Department, Hue Central Hospital, Hue City, Viet Nam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Hy Van</ForeName><Initials>HV</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Orthopaedic and Plastic Center, Hue Central Hospital, Hue City, Viet Nam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lin</LastName><ForeName>Sung-Yen</ForeName><Initials>SY</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Orthopedics, Kaohsiung Medical University Hospital, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Orthopaedic Research Center, College of Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Regenerative Medicine and Cell Therapy Research Center, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Post-Baccalaureate Medicine, College of Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chen</LastName><ForeName>Chung-Hwan</ForeName><Initials>CH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Orthopedics, Kaohsiung Medical University Hospital, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Orthopaedic Research Center, College of Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Regenerative Medicine and Cell Therapy Research Center, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Orthopedics, College of Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Ph.D. Program in Biomedical Engineering, College of Medicine, Kaohsiung Medical University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Medical Science and Technology, National Sun Yat-Sen University, Kaohsiung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Graduate Institute of Animal Vaccine Technology, College of Veterinary Medicine, National Pingtung University of Science and Technology, Pingtung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Graduate Institute of Materials Engineering, College of Engineering, National Pingtung University of Science and Technology, Pingtung, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tai</LastName><ForeName>Ta-Wei</ForeName><Initials>TW</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Orthopedics, National Cheng Kung University Hospital, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Skeleton Materials and Biocompatibility Core Lab, Research Center of Clinical Medicine, National Cheng Kung University Hospital, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, Taiwan.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2026</Year><Month>02</Month><Day>05</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Netherlands</Country><MedlineTA>Osteoporos Sarcopenia</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101666399</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2405-5255</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Artificial intelligence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Fracture risk</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Osteoporosis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Prevalence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Vietnam</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Chih-Hsing Wu has received honoraria for lectures, attending meetings, and/or travel from Eli Lilly, Roche, Amgen, Merck, Servier laboratories, GE Lunar, Harvester, TCM Biotch, Alvogen/Lotus. Tuan Van Nguyen has received a global competitive grant from Amgen and honoraria from Amgen, DKSH, and Bridge Health Care, for giving lectures and travelling to meetings. Chung-Hwan Chen received honoraria for lectures, attending meetings, and/or travel from Amgen, and Alvogen/Lotus. Ta-Wei Tai has received honoraria for lectures and attending meetings from Amgen and Alvogen/Lotus. Dat Minh Nguyen, Lan T Ho-Pham, Kim Thi Hoang Dang, and Sung-Yen Lin declare no competing interests. Use of the AI-based software (DeepXray Coxa, Alpha Intelligence Manifolds, Taiwan) was provided free of charge for academic purposes.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>16</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2025</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>27</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2026</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>28</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2026</Year><Month>4</Month><Day>13</Day><Hour>18</Hour><Minute>18</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2026</Year><Month>4</Month><Day>13</Day><Hour>18</Hour><Minute>17</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2026</Year><Month>4</Month><Day>13</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>27</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2026</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>5</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41969604</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC13069272</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.afos.2026.01.002</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S2405-5255(26)00002-6</ArticleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Reference><Citation>Salari N., Darvishi N., Bartina Y., Larti M., Kiaei A., Hemmati M., et al. Global prevalence of osteoporosis among the world older adults: a comprehensive systematic review and meta-analysis. J Orthop Surg Res. 2021;16:669.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC8590304</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">34774085</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Riggs B.L., Melton L.J., 3rd The worldwide problem of osteoporosis: insights afforded by epidemiology. Bone. 1995;17(5 Suppl):505S&#x2013;511S.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">8573428</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Tsai Y.L., Wu C.H., Li C.C., Shih C.A., Chang Y.F., Hwang J.S., et al. Drug adherence and treatment duration for denosumab and mortality risk among hip fracture patients. Osteoporos Int. 2023;34:1783&#x2013;1791.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37466659</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Tai T.W., Li C.C., Huang C.F., Chan W.P., Wu C.H. Treatment of osteoporosis after hip fracture is associated with lower all-cause mortality: