</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30704819</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kim, M.-C. et al. Independent impact of high-density lipoprotein cholesterol variability on cardiovascular disease in patients with hiv infection. <i>AIDS</i><b>32</b>, 1031&#x2013;1041 (2018).</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Longenecker, C. T. et al. Markers of inflammation and cd8 t-cell activation, but not monocyte activation, are associated with subclinical carotid artery disease in hiv-infected individuals. <i>HIV Med.</i><b>17</b>, 581&#x2013;589 (2016).
</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3640592</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23332012</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Triant, V. A. et al. Cardiovascular risk prediction functions underestimate risk in hiv infection. <i>Circulation</i><b>137</b>, 2203&#x2013;2214 (2018).
</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.117.028975</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6157923</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29444987</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mesinovic, M. <i>Cvd-hiv</i>. https://www.iapac.org/fact-sheet/hiv-and-cardiovascular-disease/ (2025).</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41494674</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>06</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>10</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2005-8330</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>27</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Jan</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Korean journal of radiology</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Korean J Radiol</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Coronary CT Angiography-Derived Fractional Flow Reserve in Asia and the United States: 2025 Status Update.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>27</StartPage><EndPage>33</EndPage><MedlinePgn>27-33</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.3348/kjr.2025.1208</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Coronary CT-derived fractional flow reserve (CT-FFR) is a noninvasive alternative to invasive FFR for assessing the hemodynamic significance of coronary artery stenosis. CT-FFR uses routinely acquired coronary CT angiography (CCTA) with artificial intelligence and computational fluid dynamics to estimate pressure gradients, thereby supporting clinical decision-making without procedural risk. Adoption and implementation vary widely across regions. In Asia, South Korea exclusively uses HeartMedi+, which enables rapid analysis, streamlined workflow integration, and high user satisfaction. Japan relies primarily on FFR<sub>CT</sub>, which has national reimbursement and significantly influences treatment strategies, reducing unnecessary invasive procedures. Hong Kong, Singapore, Taiwan, Thailand, and Vietnam remain in early or exploratory phases due to cost, reimbursement, and infrastructure barriers. In the United States, CT-FFR is guideline-endorsed and reimbursed, where HeartFlow is the most widely used, supported by robust clinical validation and outcome data. Despite regional variability, CT-FFR offers a noninvasive solution that enhances patient management, reduces unnecessary invasive testing, and is poised for broader clinical integration as technology and validation advance.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 The Korean Society of Radiology.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ahn</LastName><ForeName>Woo Jin</ForeName><Initials>WJ</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-7965-4028</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Emergency Medical Center, National Medical Center, Seoul, Republic of Korea.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ferencik</LastName><ForeName>Maros</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-5872-2223</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Knight Cardiovascular Institute, Oregon Health &amp; Science University, Portland, OR, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ng</LastName><ForeName>Ming-Yen</ForeName><Initials>MY</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-9533-3892</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Diagnostic Radiology, School of Clinical Medicine, Li Ka Shing Faculty of Medicine, The University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhong</LastName><ForeName>Liang</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-3608-4173</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>National Heart Centre Singapo