Characteristics and costs of potentially preventable inpatient stays, 2017; Healthcare Cost and Utilization Project (HCUP) Statistical Brief #259. https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb259-Potentially-Preventable-Hospitalizations-2</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">32730017</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chernozhukov V, Chetverikov D, Demirer M, et al. Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters. Econom J. 2018; 21(1): C1-C68. doi: 10.1111/ectj.12097</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1111/ectj.12097</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bach P, Chernozhukov V, Kurz M, Spindler M.. Double ML: an object-oriented implementation of double machine learning in Python. J Mach Learn Res. 2022; 53:1-6. doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2104.03220</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.48550/arXiv.2104.03220</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chernozhukov V, Cinelli C, Newey W, Sharma A, Syrgkanis V.. Long story short: omitted variable bias in causal machine learning. National Bureau of Economic Research. Published Jul 2022. doi: 10.3386/w30302</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3386/w30302</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lloyd T, Conti S, Santos F, Steventon A.. Effect on secondary care of providing enhanced support to residential and nursing home residents: a subgroup analysis of a retrospective matched cohort study. BMJ Qual Saf. 2019; 28(7): 534-546. doi: 10.1136/bmjqs-2018-009130</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1136/bmjqs-2018-009130</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC6593648</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">30956202</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ouslander JG, Lamb G, Tappen R, et al. Interventions to reduce hospitalizations from nursing homes: evaluation of the INTERACT II collaborative quality improvement project. J Am Geriatr Soc. 2011; 59(4):745-753. doi: 10.1111/j.1532-5415.2011.03333.x</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1111/j.1532-5415.2011.03333.x</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">21410447</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Maarsingh OR. The wall of evidence for continuity of care: how many more bricks do we need? Ann Fam Med. 2024; 22(3):184-186. doi: 10.1370/afm.3116</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1370/afm.3116</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC11237230</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">38806275</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Nyweide DJ, Anthony DL, Bynum JP, et al. Continuity of care and the risk of preventable hospitalization in older adults. JAMA Intern Med. 2013; 173(20): 1879-1885. doi: 10.1001/jamainternmed.2013.10059</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1001/jamainternmed.2013.10059</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC3877937</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">24043127</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Department of Health and Age Care . MyMedicare. Accessed Oct 28, 2024. https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/mymedicare</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Department of Health and Age Care . Review of general practice incentives. Accessed Oct 28, 2024. https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/review-of-general-practice-incentives</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sonmez D, Weyer G, Adelman D.. Primary care continuity, frequency, and regularity associated with Medicare savings. JAMA Netw Open. 2023; 6(8): e2329991. doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2023.29991</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2023.29991</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC10442711</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37603335</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41234615</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>14</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>16</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">2055-2076</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>11</Volume><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Season>Jan-Dec</Season></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Digital health</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Digit Health</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Wearable vital-sign sensors for early detection of severe dengue and sepsis: Clinical applications and implementation in resource-limited settings.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>20552076251396563</StartPage><MedlinePgn>20552076251396563</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">20552076251396563</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/20552076251396563</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Severe dengue and sepsis account for tens of millions of cases and deaths annually in low-resource settings, where spot-check vital sign monitoring misses early physiological deterioration. Affordable wearable sensors that stream continuous data and feed machine learning algorithms promise earlier detection and improved outcomes.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We conducted a structured narrative literature review of human studies on PubMed, Embase, Scopus, and Google Scholar that evaluated wearable vital-sign devices for predicting severe dengue or sepsis in resource-limited areas. Two reviewers independently screened, extracted, and synthesized data on study design, devices, vital streams, predictive performance, and implementation barriers.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Seven eligible studies (2019-2024) from Vietnam, Rwanda, Bangladesh, and South Korea tested photoplethysmography or single-lead electrocardiography wearables in intensive care units and emergency departments of hospitals. The algorithms achieved an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve of 0.83-0.86 for forecasting dengue shock up to 2 hours and sepsis deterioration up to 5-48&#x2005;hours before conventional recognition. The precision reached 0.79 for the three-level dengue severity classification. Continuous monitoring reduces the time to intervention and matches or exceeds the accuracy of manual charting.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Early evidence shows that low-cost wearable sensors can deliver clinically meaningful lead times for severe dengue and sepsis in developing countries' hospitals. However, generalizability, artifact suppression, power autonomy, and economic sustainability remain unproven. Multicentre pragmatic trials, edge-optimized algorithms, and full cost-effectiveness analyses are needed before routine adoption.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; The Author(s) 2025.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ahmed</LastName><ForeName>Mohamed Mustaf</ForeName><Initials>MM</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0006-5991-4052</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, SIMAD University, Mogadishu, Somalia.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">510012</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Othman</LastName><ForeName>Zhinya Kawa</ForeName><Initials>ZK</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0008-3914-0867</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Pharmacy, Kurdistan Technical Institute, Sulaymaniyah, Iraq.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Mahamed</LastName><ForeName>Sumaya Mahamud</ForeName><Initials>SM</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0008-4838-6594</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, SIMAD University, Mogadishu, Somalia.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">510012</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Okesanya</LastName><ForeName>Olalekan John</ForeName><Initials>OJ</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-3809-4271</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Public Health and Maritime Transport, University of Thessaly, Volos, Greece.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">37786</Identifier></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Medical Laboratory Science, Neuropsychiatric Hospital, Aro, Nigeria.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Musa</LastName><ForeName>Shuaibu Saidu</ForeName><Initials>SS</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-0138-3261</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>School of Global Health, Faculty of Medicine, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok, Thailand.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">26683</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Branda</LastName><ForeName>Francesco</ForeName><Initials>F</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-9485-3877</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Unit of Medical Statistics and Molecular Epidemiology, Campus Bio-Medico University of Rome, Rome, Italy.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">9317</Identifier></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lucero-Prisno</LastName><ForeName>Don Eliseo</ForeName><Initials>DE</Initials><Suffix>3rd</Suffix><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2179-6365</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Global Health and Development, London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, London, UK.</Affiliation><Identifier Source="RINGGOLD">4906</Identifier></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Center for Research and Development, Cebu Normal University, Cebu, Philippines.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Research Unit, Bukidnon State University, Malaybalay City, Philippines.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016454">Review</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>11</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>United States</Country><MedlineTA>Digit Health</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>101690863</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>2055-2076</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Wearable sensors</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">dengue</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">early warning</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">low-resource settings</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">sepsis</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">vital-sign monitoring</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>The authors declared no potential conflicts of interest with respect to the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>7</Month><Day>20</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>28</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>14</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>29</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>14</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>28</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>14</Day><Hour>4</Hour><Minute>38</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pmc-release"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>11</Day></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>epublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41234615</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12605882</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1177/20552076251396563</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">10.1177