in, &#x201c;Fluoride to Stay in Bega Valley Shire's Water Supply,&#x201d; About Regional Website (2019), https://aboutregional.com.au/fluoride&#x2010;to&#x2010;stay&#x2010;in&#x2010;bega&#x2010;valley&#x2010;shires&#x2010;water&#x2010;supply/.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>National Health and Medical Research Council, &#x201c;NHMRC Public Statement 2017: Water Fluoridation and Human Health in Australia,&#x201d; NHMRC Website (2017), https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/about&#x2010;us/publications/nhmrc&#x2010;public&#x2010;statement&#x2010;water&#x2010;fluoridation&#x2010;and&#x2010;human&#x2010;health.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>J. M. Armfield and H. F. Akers, &#x201c;Risk Perception and Water Fluoridation Support and Opposition in Australia,&#x201d; Journal of Public Health Dentistry 71, no. 4 (2011): 356&#x2013;363, https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1752&#x2010;7325.2011.00272.x.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Public Health England, &#x201c;Water Fluoridation: Health Monitoring Report for England 2018,&#x201d; GOV.UK Website (2018), https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/water&#x2010;fluoridation&#x2010;health&#x2010;monitoring&#x2010;report&#x2010;for&#x2010;england&#x2010;2018.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>UK Government, &#x201c;Health and Care Bill to Introduce Water Fluoridation Schemes,&#x201d; GOV.UK Website (2021), https://www.gov.uk/government/news/health&#x2010;and&#x2010;care&#x2010;bill&#x2010;to&#x2010;introduce&#x2010;water&#x2010;fluoridation&#x2010;schemes.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>R. Harriman and M. Patel, &#x201c;Anti&#x2010;Fluoridation Social Media Campaigns: A Narrative Review of Strategies and Tactics,&#x201d; Public Health 203 (2022): 97&#x2013;105, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.puhe.2021.11.019.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>&#x201c;Brazil's New President and Public Health,&#x201d; Lancet 393, no. 10166 (2019): 292, https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140&#x2010;6736(19)30155&#x2010;3.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>P. Fraz&#xe3;o and P. C. Narvai, &#x201c;Water Fluoridation in Brazilian Cities at the First Decade of the 21st Century,&#x201d; Revista de Sa&#xfa;de P&#xfa;blica 51 (2017): 47, https://doi.org/10.1590/S1518&#x2010;8787.2017051006779.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>G. A. Pucca, Jr., M. Gabriel, M. E. de Araujo, and F. C. de Almeida, &#x201c;Ten Years of a National Oral Health Policy in Brazil: Innovation, Boldness, and Numerous Challenges,&#x201d; Journal of Dental Research 94, no. 10 (2015): 1333&#x2013;1337, https://doi.org/10.1177/0022034515603997.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>B. Seymour, R. Getman, A. Saraf, et&#xa0;al., &#x201c;When Advocacy Obscures Accuracy Online: Digital Pandemics of Public Health Misinformation Through an Anti&#x2010;Fluoridation Facebook Group,&#x201d; American Journal of Public Health 105, no. 3 (2015): 517&#x2013;523, https://doi.org/10.2105/AJPH.2014.302460.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>&#x201c;Trump's Medicaid Disruption Threatens Vital Resource for Millions,&#x201d; The Guardian (2025), https://www.theguardian.com/us&#x2010;news/2025/jan/29/medicaid&#x2010;trump&#x2010;funding.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>K. Wagatsuma, &#x201c;Implications of President Trump's Second Term Executive Orders on Global and Public Health,&#x201d; International Journal of Public Health 70 (2025): 70, https://doi.org/10.3389/ijph.2025.1608402.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>NBC New York, &#x201c;Trump Says RFK Jr.'s Plan to Remove Fluoride From Public Water &#x2018;Sounds Okay to Me&#x2019;,&#x201d; accessed November 07, 2024, https://www.nbcnewyork.com/news/business/money&#x2010;report/trump&#x2010;says&#x2010;rfk&#x2010;jr&#x2010;plan&#x2010;to&#x2010;remove&#x2010;fluoride&#x2010;from&#x2010;public&#x2010;water&#x2010;sounds&#x2010;okay&#x2010;to&#x2010;me/5949596/?utm_source=chatgpt.com.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>D. Hickey, D. M. T. Fessler, K. Lerman, and K. Burghardt, &#x201c;X Under Musk's Leadership: Substantial Hate and No Reduction in Inauthentic Activity,&#x201d; PLoS One 20, no. 2 (2025): e0313293, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0313293.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>D. Higgins and N. Nassif, &#x201c;Public Perceptions of Water Fluoridation in NSW Australia: A Media Analysis,&#x201d; Health Promotion Journal of Australia 31, no. 1 (2020): 106&#x2013;111, https://doi.org/10.1002/hpja.281.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>A. Gesser&#x2010;Edelsburg, A. Diamant, R. Hijazi, and G. S. Mesch, &#x201c;Correcting Misinformation by Health Organizations During Measles Outbreaks: A Controlled Experiment,&#x201d; PLoS One 13, no. 12 (2018): e0209505, https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0209505.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>C. Umunyana, G. Tuyizere, and A. Mbarushimana, &#x201c;Students' Sentiment Analysis Using Natural Language Toolkit in Machine Learning for Module Evaluation,&#x201d; European Journal of Engineering and Technology Research 9, no. 1 (2024): 72&#x2013;75, https://doi.org/10.24018/ejeng.2024.9.1.3006.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41120019</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>03</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>27</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1096-1186</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>221</Volume><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Nov</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Pharmacological research</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Pharmacol Res</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Reimagining ethnopharmacology with generative AI: Towards inclusive, ethical, and data-driven traditional medicine.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>108002</StartPage><MedlinePgn>108002</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.phrs.2025.108002</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1043-6618(25)00427-X</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Ethnopharmacology explores bioactive compounds rooted in traditional medical knowledge systems and holds immense promise for drug discovery, cultural preservation, and healthcare innovation. However, fragmented documentation, minimal digitization, and limited integration with biomedical frameworks remain major barriers. The advent of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI), including large language models (LLMs) and molecular generation algorithms, offers transformative solutions to these challenges. This narrative review critically examines the application of GenAI in ethnopharmacology and highlights its role in digitizing traditional knowledge, decoding polyherbal formulations, predicting herb-drug interactions, and accelerating phytopharmaceutical discovery. It synthesizes current literature on GenAI tools and methods relevant to ethnopharmacology, considering natural language processing, knowledge graph construction, molecular modeling, and multimodal data integration. A five-phase strategic framework is proposed for the ethical and effective implementation of GenAI. This review narrates real-world applications from Asian (Ayurvedic, Chinese, Japanese, Thai, Vietnamese), African, and Indigenous American medicines systems demonstrate adaptability across cultures. Stakeholder-specific benefits, spanning academia, healthcare, industry, and indigenous communities, are also discussed, along with methodological innovations and ethical considerations. GenAI offers a significant transition in ethnopharmacology by integrating traditional knowledge systems with advanced computational tools to develop inclusive data-driven innovation across global traditional medicine systems.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2025. Published by Elsevier Ltd.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bhadra</LastName><ForeName>Santanu</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Society for Ethnopharmacology, 23/3 Shaktigarh, Jadavpur, Kolkata 700032, India. Electronic address: sanbhadra@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pundir</LastName><ForeName>Charu</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Society for Ethnopharmacology, 23/3 Shaktigarh, Jadavpur, Kolkata 700032, India. Electronic address: c.pundir13@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Mukherjee</LastName><ForeName>Maria</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Society for Ethnopharmacology, 23/3 Shaktigarh, Jadavpur, Kolkata 700032, India. Electronic address: mukherjeemaria@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kar</LastName><ForeName>Amit</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Society for Ethnopharmacology, 23/3 Shaktigarh, Jadavpur, Kolkata 700032, India. Electronic address: amit.kar2@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Banerjee</LastName><ForeName>Subhadip</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Medicinal Plant Innovation Centre, Mae Fah Luang University, 333 Moo1, Thasud, Muang, Chiang Rai 57100, Thailand. Electronic address: amisubhadipbanerjee@gmail.com.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Charoensup</LastName><ForeName>Rawiwan</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Medicinal Plant Innovation Centre, Mae Fah Luang University, 333 Moo1, Thasud, Muang, Chiang Rai 57100, Thailand. Electronic address: rawiwan.cha@mfu.ac.th.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Duangyod</LastName><ForeName>Thidarat</ForeName><Initials>T</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Medicinal Plant Innovation Centre, Mae Fah Luang University, 333 Moo1, Thasud, Muang, Chiang Rai 57100, Thailand. Electronic address: thidarat.dua@mfu.ac.th.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Mukherjee</LastName><ForeName>Pulok K</ForeName><Initials>PK</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Society for Ethnopharmacology, 23/3 Shaktigarh, Jadavpur, Kolkata 700032, India; School of Natural Product Studies, Department of Pharmaceutical Technology, Jadavpur University, Kolkata 700032, India. Electronic address: pulok.mukherjee@jadavpuruniversity.in.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016454">Review</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>19</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Netherlands</Country><MedlineTA>Pharmacol Res</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>8907422</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>1043-6618</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D006801" MajorTopicYN="N">Humans</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001185" MajorTopicYN="Y">Artificial Intelligence</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000941" MajorTopicYN="N">ethics</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D008519" MajorTopicYN="Y">Medicine, Traditional</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D020834" MajorTopicYN="Y">Ethnopharmacology</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000941" MajorTopicYN="N">ethics</QualifierName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000818" MajorTopicYN="N">Animals</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D055808" MajorTopicYN="N">Drug Discovery</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000379" MajorTopicYN="N">methods</QualifierName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Data-driven innovation</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Drug discovery</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Ethnopharmacology</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Generative AI</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Indigenous knowledge</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Natural language processing</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Traditional medicine</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of Competing Interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>6</Month><Day>7</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>16</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>18</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2025</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>4</Day><Hour>0</Hour><Minute>28</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>22</Day><Hour>2</Hour><Minute>35</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>21</Day><Hour>19</Hour><Minute>21</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">41120019</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.phrs.2025.108002</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S1043-6618(25)00427-X</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">41064697</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>09</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>25</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">2328-8957</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>12</Volume><Issue>10</Issue><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Oct</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Open forum infectious diseases</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Open Forum Infect Dis</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Accelerating Cough-Based Algorithms for Pulmonary Tuberculosis Screening: Results From the CODA TB DREAM Challenge.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>ofaf572</StartPage><MedlinePgn>ofaf572</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">ofaf572</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1093/ofid/ofaf572</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Open-access data challenges can accelerate innovation in artificial intelligence-based tools. In the Cough Diagnostic Algorithm for Tuberculosis (CODA TB) DREAM Challenge, we developed and independently validated cough sound-based artificial intelligence algorithms for tuberculosis screening.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">We included data from 2143 adults with &#x2265;2 weeks of cough from outpatient clinics in India, Madagascar, the Philippines, South Africa, Tanzania, Uganda, and Vietnam. A standard tuberculosis evaluation was completed, and &#x2265;3 solicited coughs were recorded using a smartphone. We invited teams to develop models using training data to classify microbiologically confirmed tuberculosis disease using (1) cough sound features only and/or (2) cough sound features with routinely available clinical data. After 4 months, they submitted the algorithms for independent test set validation. Models were ranked by area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) and partial AUROC (pAUROC) to achieve at least 80% sensitivity and 60% specificity.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">Eleven cough models and 6 cough-plus-clinical models were submitted. AUROCs for cough models ranged from 0.69 to 0.74, and the highest performing model achieved 55.5% specificity (95% confidence interval, 47.7%-64.2%) at 80% sensitivity. The addition of clinical data improved AUROCs (range, 0.78-0.83); 5 of the 6 models reached the target pAUROC, and the highest performing model had 73.8% specificity (95% confidence interval, 60.8%-80.0%) at 80% sensitivity. The AUROC varied by country and was higher among male and human immunodeficiency virus-negative individuals.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">In a short period, an open-access data challenge facilitated the development of new cough-based tuberculosis algorithms and demonstrated potential as a tuberculosis screening tool.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; The Author(s) 2025. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of Infectious Diseases Society of America.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Jaganath</LastName><ForeName>Devan</ForeName><Initials>D</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8555-5667</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Division of Pediatric Infectious Diseases, University of California, San Francisco, California, San Francisco, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Center for Tuberculosis, University of California, San Francisco, California, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sieberts</LastName><ForeName>Solveig K</ForeName><Initials>SK</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0003-1033-0954</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, Washington, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Raberahona</LastName><ForeName>Mihaja</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-8857-5834</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>CHU Joseph Rasera Befelatanana, Antananarivo, Madagascar.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre D'Infectiologie Charles M&#xe9;rieux, Universit&#xe9; D'Antananarivo, Antananarivo, Madagascar.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Huddart</LastName><ForeName>Sophie</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-6425-3371</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Center for Tuberculosis, University of California, San Francisco, California, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Epidemiology and Biostatistics, University of California, San Francisco, San Francisco, California, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Omberg</LastName><ForeName>Larsson</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Sage Bionetworks, Seattle, Washington, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Rakotoarivelo</LastName><ForeName>Rivo</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>CHU Tambohobe Fianarantsoa, Haute-Matsiatra, Madagascar.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Universit&#xe9; de Fianarantsoa, Fianarantsoa, Madagascar.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lyimo</LastName><ForeName>Issa</ForeName><Initials>I</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-5804-7352</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Environmental and Ecological Sciences &amp; Interventions and Clinical Trials Departments, Ifakara Health Institute, Dar es Salaam, Tanzania.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lweno</LastName><ForeName>Omar</ForeName><Initials>O</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Environmental and Ecological Sciences &amp; Interventions and Clinical Trials Departments, Ifakara Health Institute, Dar es Salaam, Tanzania.</Affiliation></AffiliationI