. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>2021 SPIRIT template. Alliance for Healthy Cities. 2021.  [12-05-2024]. https://www.alliance-healthycities.com/docs/AFHCAward2021/AFHC%20Awards%202021_SPIRIT_TEMPLATE.docx URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Members. Alliance for Healthy Cities.  [03-03-2024]. https://www.alliance-healthycities.com/htmls/members/index_members.html URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Data bank. World Bank Group.  [14-01-2025]. https://databank.worldbank.org/ URL. Accessed.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ramaswami A, Russell AG, Culligan PJ, Sharma KR, Kumar E. Meta-principles for developing smart, sustainable, and healthy cities. Science. 2016 May 20;352(6288):940&#x2013;943. doi: 10.1126/science.aaf7160. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1126/science.aaf7160</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">27199418</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Zou H. 12th International Conference on Computational Intelligence and Security (CIS) IEEE Publications; 2016. Protection of personal information security in the age of big data; pp. 586&#x2013;589. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1109/CIS.2016.0142</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lee H, Chung MA, Kim H, Nam EW. The effect of cognitive function health care using artificial intelligence robots for older adults: systematic review and meta-analysis. JMIR Aging. 2022 Jun 28;5(2):e38896. doi: 10.2196/38896. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.2196/38896</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC9277531</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">35672268</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>de Leeuw E. Global and local (glocal) health: the WHO Healthy Cities programme. Global Change and Human Health. 2001;2(1):34&#x2013;45. doi: 10.1023/A:1011991014805. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1023/A:1011991014805</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Longoria IAI, Bustamante-Bello R, Ram&#xed;rez-Montoya MS, Molina A. Systematic mapping of digital gap and gender, age, ethnicity, or disability. Sustainability. 2022;14(3):1297. doi: 10.3390/su14031297. doi.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3390/su14031297</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lee H, Lim JA, Nam HK. Effect of a digital literacy program on older adults&#x2019; digital social behavior: a quasi-experimental study. Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2022 Sep 29;19(19):12404. doi: 10.3390/ijerph191912404. doi. Medline.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3390/ijerph191912404</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC9564917</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">36231707</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">39922126</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2025</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>06</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>22</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1879-0852</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>218</Volume><PubDate><Year>2025</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>11</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>European journal of cancer (Oxford, England : 1990)</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Eur J Cancer</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Generative AI chatbots for reliable cancer information: Evaluating web-search, multilingual, and reference capabilities of emerging large language models.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>115274</StartPage><MedlinePgn>115274</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejca.2025.115274</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0959-8049(25)00055-3</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Recent advancements in large language models (LLMs) enable real-time web search, improved referencing, and multilingual support, yet ensuring they provide safe health information remains crucial. This perspective evaluates seven publicly accessible LLMs-ChatGPT, Co-Pilot, Gemini, MetaAI, Claude, Grok, Perplexity-on three simple cancer-related queries across eight languages (336 responses: English, French, Chinese, Thai, Hindi, Nepali, Vietnamese, and Arabic). None of the 42 English responses contained clinically meaningful hallucinations, whereas 7 of 294 non-English responses did. 48&#x202f;% (162/336) of responses included valid references, but 39&#x202f;% of the English references were.com links reflecting quality concerns. English responses frequently exceeded an eighth-grade level, and many non-English outputs were also complex. These findings reflect substantial progress over the past 2-years but reveal persistent gaps in multilingual accuracy, reliable reference inclusion, referral practices, and readability. Ongoing benchmarking is essential to ensure LLMs safely support global health information dichotomy and meet online information standards.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd.. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Menz</LastName><ForeName>Bradley D</ForeName><Initials>BD</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Modi</LastName><ForeName>Natansh D</ForeName><Initials>ND</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Abuhelwa</LastName><ForeName>Ahmad Y</ForeName><Initials>AY</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Pharmacy Practice and Pharmacotherapeutics, College of Pharmacy, University of Sharjah, Sharjah, United Arab Emirates.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ruanglertboon</LastName><ForeName>Warit</ForeName><Initials>W</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Division of Health and Applied Sciences, Prince of Songkla University, Songkhla, Thailand; Research Center in Mathematics and Statistics with Applications, Discipline of Statistics, Division of Computational Science, Faculty of Science, Prince of Songkla University, Songkhla, Thailand.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Vitry</LastName><ForeName>Agnes</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>University of South Australia, Clinical and Health Sciences, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Gao</LastName><ForeName>Yuan</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Li</LastName><ForeName>Lee X</ForeName><Initials>LX</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chhetri</LastName><ForeName>Rakchha</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Chu</LastName><ForeName>Bianca</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Bacchi</LastName><ForeName>Stephen</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Neurology and the Center for Genomic Medicine, Massachusetts General Hospital and Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02138, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Kichenadasse</LastName><ForeName>Ganessan</ForeName><Initials>G</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia; Flinders Centre for Innovation in Cancer, Department of Medical Oncology, Flinders Medical Centre, Flinders University, Bedford Park, South Australia, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Shahnam</LastName><ForeName>Adel</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Medical Oncology, Peter MacCallum Cancer Centre, Melbourne, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Rowland</LastName><ForeName>Andrew</ForeName><Initials>A</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sorich</LastName><ForeName>Michael J</ForeName><Initials>MJ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hopkins</LastName><ForeName>Ashley M</ForeName><Initials>AM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>College of Medicine and Public Health, Flinders Health and Medical Research Institute, Flinders University, Adelaide, Australia. Electronic address: ashley.hopkins@flinders.edu.au.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2025</Year><Month>02</Month><Day>03</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>England</Country><MedlineTA>Eur J Cancer</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>9005373</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0959-8049</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D006801" MajorTopicYN="N">Humans</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D009369" MajorTopicYN="Y">Neoplasms</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D020407" MajorTopicYN="Y">Internet</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D019303" MajorTopicYN="Y">Multilingualism</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D001185" MajorTopicYN="Y">Artificial Intelligence</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D054626" MajorTopicYN="Y">Consumer Health Information</DescriptorName><QualifierName UI="Q000592" MajorTopicYN="N">standards</QualifierName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000098342" MajorTopicYN="N">Large Language Models</DescriptorName></MeshHeading><MeshHeading><DescriptorName UI="D000098842" MajorTopicYN="N">Generative Artificial Intelligence</DescriptorName></MeshHeading></MeshHeadingList><KeywordList Owner="NOTNLM"><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Artificial intelligence</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Cancer enquiries</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">English</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Health enquiries</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Language</Keyword><Keyword MajorTopicYN="N">Large language model</Keyword></KeywordList><CoiStatement>Declaration of Competing Interest The authors declare the following financial interests/personal relationships which may be considered as potential competing interests: A.R and M.J.S are recipients of investigator-initiated funding for research outside the scope of the current study from AstraZeneca, Boehringer Ingelheim, Pfizer and Takeda. A.R is a recipient of speaker fees from Boehringer Ingelheim and Genentech. The author team have no other potential conflicts of interest with respect to this research and/or publication to declare.</CoiStatement></MedlineCitation><PubmedData><History><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="received"><Year>2025</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>5</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="revised"><Year>2025</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>22</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="accepted"><Year>2025</Year><Month>1</Month><Day>24</Day></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="medline"><Year>2025</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>27</Day><Hour>5</Hour><Minute>1</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="pubmed"><Year>2025</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>9</Day><Hour>6</Hour><Minute>20</Minute></PubMedPubDate><PubMedPubDate PubStatus="entrez"><Year>2025</Year><Month>2</Month><Day>8</Day><Hour>18</Hour><Minute>9</Minute></PubMedPubDate></History><PublicationStatus>ppublish</PublicationStatus><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">39922126</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.ejca.2025.115274</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pii">S0959-8049(25)00055-3</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="PubMed-not-MEDLINE" Owner="NLM"><PMID Version="1">39734422</PMID><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>04</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic-eCollection"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2631-7745</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>17</Volume><PubDate><Year>2024</Year><Season>Jan-Dec</Season></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Therapeutic advances in gastrointestinal endoscopy</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Ther Adv Gastrointest Endosc</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Application of artificial intelligence in gastrointestinal endoscopy in Vietnam: a narrative review.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>26317745241306562</StartPage><MedlinePgn>26317745241306562</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">26317745241306562</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/26317745241306562</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The utilization of artificial intelligence (AI) in gastrointestinal (GI) endoscopy has witnessed significant progress and promising results in recent years worldwide. From 2019 to 2023, the European Society of Gastrointestinal Endoscopy has released multiple guidelines/consensus with recommendations on integrating AI for detecting and classifying lesions in practical endoscopy. In Vietnam, since 2019, several preliminary studies have been conducted to develop AI algorithms for GI endoscopy, focusing on lesion detection. These studies have yielded high accuracy results ranging from 86% to 92%. For upper GI endoscopy, ongoing research directions comprise image quality assessment, detection of anatomical landmarks, simulating image-enhanced endoscopy, and semi-automated tools supporting the delineation of GI lesions on endoscopic images. For lower GI endoscopy, most studies focus on developing AI algorithms for colorectal polyps' detection and classification based on the risk of malignancy. In conclusion, the application of AI in this field represents a promising research direction, presenting challenges and opportunities for real-world implementation within the Vietnamese healthcare context.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; The Author(s), 2024.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dao</LastName><ForeName>Hang Viet</ForeName><Initials>HV</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Research and Education Department, Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, 09 Dao Duy Anh Street, Dong Da District, Hanoi City, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Internal Medicine, Hanoi Medical University, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Endoscopy Center, Hanoi Medical University Hospital, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Binh Phuc</ForeName><Initials>BP</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Tung Thanh</ForeName><Initials>TT</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0009-6943-7378</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lam</LastName><ForeName>Hoa Ngoc</ForeName><Initials>HN</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2256-5016</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Trang Thi Huyen</ForeName><Initials>TTH</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0001-0453-0426</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dang</LastName><ForeName>Thao Thi</ForeName><Initials>TT</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Hoang</LastName><ForeName>Long Bao</ForeName><Initials>LB</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-2829-3875</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Institute of Gastroenterology and Hepatology, Hanoi, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Le</LastName><ForeName>Hung Quang</ForeName><Initials>HQ</Ini