Ministry of Health. Decision 5316 QD-BYT digital transformation program in healthcare until 2025, https://thuvienphapluat.vn/van-ban/The-thao-Y-te/Quyet-dinh-5316-QD-BYT-2020-phe-duyet-chuong-trinh-chuyen-doi-so-y-te-den-2025-460152.aspx (2020, accessed 1 October 2024).</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Mori Y, East JE, Hassan C, et al. Benefits and challenges in implementation of artificial intelligence in colonoscopy: world endoscopy organization position statement. Dig Endosc 2023; 35(4): 422&#x2013;429.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pmc">PMC12136278</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">36749036</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Hang DV, Trang NTH, Uyen VTT, et al. Experiences and acceptance of endocsopists regarding the implementation of an AI algorithim intergrated system in gastrointestinal endoscopy in Vietnam. Vietnam Med J 2024; 542(3): 272&#x2013;276.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ahmad OF, Stoyanov D, Lovat LB. Barriers and pitfalls for artificial intelligence in gastroenterology: ethical and regulatory issues. Tech Innovations Gastrointestinal Endoscopy 2020; 22(2): 80&#x2013;84.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lee J, QUEK SXZ, KOO CS, et al. Survey on the perceptions of Asian endoscopists to artificial intelligence. Preprint. Research Square.2024.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">39714636</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2024</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>23</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>21</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1573-2959</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>197</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2024</Year><Month>Dec</Month><Day>23</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Environmental monitoring and assessment</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Environ Monit Assess</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Mapping of high-resolution daily particulate matter (PM<sub>2.5</sub>) concentration at the city level through a machine learning-based downscaling approach.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>94</StartPage><MedlinePgn>94</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s10661-024-13562-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>PM<sub>2.5</sub> pollution is a major global concern, especially in Vietnam, due to its harmful effects on health and the environment. Monitoring local PM<sub>2.5</sub> levels is crucial for assessing air quality. However, Vietnam's state-of-the-art (SOTA) dataset with a 3&#xa0;km resolution needs to be revised to depict spatial variation in smaller regions accurately. In this research, we investigated machine learning-based downscaling methods to improve the spatial resolution and quality of Vietnam's existing 3&#xa0;km PM<sub>2.5</sub> products using different approaches: traditional machine learning models (random forest, XGBoost, Catboost, support vector regression (SVR), mixed effect model (MEM)) and deep learning models (long short-term memory (LSTM), convolutional neural network (CNN), convolutional LSTM (ConvLSTM)). Overall, the CatBoost 2-day lag model exhibited superior performance. In terms of modeling, integrating temporal factors into tree-based models can enhance predictive accuracy. Furthermore, when faced with small datasets, traditional machine learning models demonstrate superior performance over complex deep learning approaches. The validation of machine and deep learning models based on their PM<sub>2.5</sub> generated maps is requested because these models can obtain very high results for model evaluation but are unrealistic for application. In this study, compared to the state-of-the-art (SOTA) PM<sub>2.5</sub> maps in Vietnam and the SOTA global maps, the proposed CatBoost 2-day lag model's maps showed a 57% increase in the correlation coefficient (Pearson R), as well as 42-73%, 28-75%, and 39-75% reductions in root mean squared error (RMSE), mean relative error (MRE), and mean absolute error (MAE), respectively. Additionally, the daily, monthly, and year-average maps generated by the Catboost 2-day lag model effectively capture the spatial distribution and seasonal variations of PM<sub>2.5</sub> in Ho Chi Minh City. These findings indicate a substantial enhancement in the accuracy and reliability of downscaled PM<sub>2.5</sub> maps.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#xa9; 2024. The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature Switzerland AG.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Phuong D M</ForeName><Initials>PDM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Information Technology, University of Engineering and Technology, Vietnam National University Hanoi, E3 Building, 144 Xuan Thuy Street, Dich Vong Hau Ward, Cau Giay District, Ha Noi, 100000, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Phan</LastName><ForeName>An H</ForeName><Initials>AH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Information Technology, University of Engineering and Technology, Vietnam National University Hanoi, E3 Building, 144 Xuan Thuy Street, Dich Vong Hau Ward, Cau Giay District, Ha Noi, 100000, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ngo</LastName><ForeName>Truong X</ForeName><Initials>TX</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Information Technology, University of Engineering and Technology, Vietnam National University Hanoi, E3 Building, 144 Xuan Thuy Street, Dich Vong Hau Ward, Cau Giay District, Ha Noi, 100000, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ho</LastName><ForeName>Bang Q</ForeName><Initials>BQ</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Academic Affairs, Vietnam National University, 142 To Hien Thanh St, District 10, Ho Chi Minh City, 700000, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Pham</LastName><ForeName>Tran Vu</ForeName><Initials>TV</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Computer Science and Engineering, Ho Chi Minh City University of Technology (HCMUT), VNU-HCM, Ho Chi Minh City, 700000, Vietnam.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Nguyen</LastName><ForeName>Thanh T N</ForeName><Initials>TTN</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Faculty of Information Technology, University of Engineering and Technology, Vietnam National University Hanoi, E3 Building, 144 Xuan Thuy Street, Dich Vong Hau Ward, Cau Giay District, Ha Noi, 100000, Vietnam. thanhntn@vnu.edu.vn.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>KC-4.0-25/19-25</GrantID><Agency>Ministry of Science and Technology</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>KC-4.0-25/19-25</GrantID><Agency>Ministry of Science and Technology</Agency><Country/></Grant><Grant><GrantID>KC-4.0-25/19-25</GrantID><Agency>Ministry of Science and Technology</Agency><Country/></Grant></GrantList><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList><ArticleDate DateType="Electronic"><Year>2024</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>23</Day></ArticleDate></Article><MedlineJournalInfo><Country>Netherlands</Country><MedlineTA>Environ Monit Assess</MedlineTA><NlmUniqueID>8508350</NlmUniqueID><ISSNLinking>0167-6369</ISSNLinking></MedlineJournalInfo><ChemicalList><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D052638">Particulate Matter</NameOfSubstance></Chemical><Chemical><RegistryNumber>0</RegistryNumber><NameOfSubstance UI="D000393">Air Pollutants</NameOfSubstance></Chemical></ChemicalList><CitationSubset>IM</CitationSubset><MeshHeadingList><MeshHeading><