ecision making for sustainable development: An overview of applications. J. Clean. Prod. 2019;212:119&#x2013;138. doi: 10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.11.270.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.11.270</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chelan M.M., Alijanpour A., Barani H., Motamedi J., Azadi H., Passel S.V. Economic sustainability assessment in semi-steppe rangelands. Sci. Total Environ. 2018;637&#x2013;638:112&#x2013;119. doi: 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.04.428.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.scitotenv.2018.04.428</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">29751294</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Grodach C. Barriers to sustainable economic development: The Dallas-Fort Worth experience. Cities. 2011;28:300&#x2013;309. doi: 10.1016/j.cities.2011.02.006.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.cities.2011.02.006</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Baumg&#xe4;rtner S., Quaas M. What is sustainability economics? Ecol. Econ. 2010;69:445&#x2013;450. doi: 10.1016/j.ecolecon.2009.11.019.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.ecolecon.2009.11.019</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Sachs I. Social Sustainability and Whole Development: Exploring the Dimensions of Sustainable Development. Zed Book; London, UK: 1999.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chiu R.L.H. Social Sustainability, Sustainable Development and Housing Development: The Experience of Hong Kong. In: Forrest R., Lee J., editors. Housing and Social Change: East-West Perspectives. Routledge; New York, NY, USA: 2003. pp. 221&#x2013;239.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Murphy K. The social pillar of sustainable development: A literature review and framework for policy analysis. Sustain. Sci. Pract. Policy. 2012;8:15&#x2013;29. doi: 10.1080/15487733.2012.11908081.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1080/15487733.2012.11908081</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Davoodi S., Fallah H., Aliabadi M. Determination of Affective Critrions on Social Sustainability in Architectural Design. Current Trends in Technology and science; Proceedings of the 8th SAS Tech 2014 Symposium on Advances in Science &amp; Technology-Commission-IV; Mashhad, Iran. 13 February 2014.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Gazzola P., Campo A.G.D., Onyango V. Going green vs. going smart for sustainable development: Quo vadis? J. Clean. Prod. 2019;214:881&#x2013;892. doi: 10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.12.234.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.12.234</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Verma P., Raghubanshi A.S. Urban sustainability indicators: Challenges and opportunities. Ecol. Indic. 2018;93:282&#x2013;291. doi: 10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.05.007.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.ecolind.2018.05.007</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Ameen R.F.M., Mourshed M. Urban sustainability assessment framework development: The ranking and weighting of sustainability indicators using analytic hierarchy process. Sustain. Cities Soc. 2019;44:356&#x2013;366. doi: 10.1016/j.scs.2018.10.020.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.scs.2018.10.020</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Moroke T., Schoeman C., Schoeman I. Developing a neighborhood sustainability assessment method: An approach to sustainable urban development. Sustain. Cities Soc. 2019 forthcoming.</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kaur H., Garg P. Urban sustainability assessment tools: A review. J. Clean. Prod. 2019;210:146&#x2013;158. doi: 10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.11.009.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.jclepro.2018.11.009</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Atanda J.O. Developing a social sustainability assessment framework. Sustain. Cities Soc. 2019;44:237&#x2013;252. doi: 10.1016/j.scs.2018.09.023.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/j.scs.2018.09.023</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Saaty T.L. How to make a decision: The analytic hierarchy process. Eur. J. Oper. Res. 1990;48:9&#x2013;26. doi: 10.1016/0377-2217(90)90057-I.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/0377-2217(90)90057-I</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11659401</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chang D.Y. Applications of the extent analysis method on fuzzy AHP. Eur. J. Oper. Res. 1996;95:649&#x2013;655. doi: 10.1016/0377-2217(95)00300-2.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1016/0377-2217(95)00300-2</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Shaverdi M., Ramezani I., Tahmasebi R., Rostamy A.A.A. Combining fuzzy AHP and fuzzy TOPSIS with financial ratios to design a novel performance evaluation model. Int. J. Fuzzy Syst. 2016;18:248&#x2013;262. doi: 10.1007/s40815-016-0142-8.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1007/s40815-016-0142-8</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Chou Y.C., Yen H.Y., Dang V.T., Sun C.C. Assessing the human resource in science and technology for Asian countries: Application of fuzzy AHP and fuzzy TOPSIS. Symmetry. 2019;11:251&#x2013;267. doi: 10.3390/sym11020251.</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3390/sym11020251</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>United Nations . The Millennium Development Goals Report 2015. UN; New York, NY, USA: 2015.</Citation></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">31253823</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2020</Year><Month>10</Month><Day>23</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2025</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>30</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2045-2322</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>9</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2019</Year><Month>Jun</Month><Day>28</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Scientific reports</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Sci Rep</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Cross-serotype interactions and disease outcome prediction of dengue infections in Vietnam.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>9395</StartPage><MedlinePgn>9395</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">9395</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41598-019-45816-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Dengue pathogenesis is extremely complex. Dengue infections are thought to induce life-long immunity from homologous challenges as well as a multi-factorial heterologous risk enhancement. Here, we use the data collected from a prospective cohort study of dengue infections in schoolchildren in Vietnam to disentangle how serotype interactions modulate clinical disease risk in the year following serum collection. We use multinomial logistic regression to correlate the yearly neutralizing antibody measurements obtained with each infecting serotype in all dengue clinical cases collected over the course of 6 years (2004-2009). This allowed us to extrapolate a fully discretised matrix of serotype interactions, revealing clear signals of increased risk of clinical illness in individuals primed with a previous dengue infection. The sequences of infections which produced a higher risk of dengue fever upon secondary infection are: DEN1 followed by DEN2; DEN1 followed by DEN4; DEN2 followed by DEN3; and DEN4 followed by DEN3. We also used this longitudinal data to train a machine learning algorithm on antibody titre differences between consecutive years to unveil asymptomatic dengue infections and estimate asymptomatic infection to clinical case ratios over time, allowing for a better characterisation of the population's past exposure to different serotypes.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Aguas</LastName><ForeName>R</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MRC Centre for Global Infectious Disease Analysis, School of Public Health, Imperial College London, Faculty of Medicine, Norfolk Place, London, W2 1PG, UK. r.aguas@imperial.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Centre for Tropical Medicine and Global Health, Nuffield Department of Medicine, University of Oxford, Oxford, United Kingdom. r.aguas@imperial.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Mahidol-Oxford Tropical Medicine Research Unit, Faculty of Tropical Medicine, Mahidol University, Bangkok, Thailand. r.aguas@imperial.ac.uk.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Dorigatti</LastName><ForeName>I</ForeName><Initials>I</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0001-9959-0706</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MRC Centre for Global Infectious Disease Analysis, School of Public Health, Imperial College London, Faculty of Medicine, Norfolk Place, London, W2 1PG, UK.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Coudeville</LastName><ForeName>L</ForeName><Initials>L</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Sanofi Pasteur, 14 espace Henry Vall&#xe9;e, 69007, Lyon, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Luxemburger</LastName><ForeName>C</ForeName><Initials>C</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Sanofi Pasteur, 14 espace Henry Vall&#xe9;e, 69007, Lyon, France.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Ferguson</LastName><ForeName>N M</ForeName><Initials>NM</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-1154-8093</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>MRC Centre for Global Infectious Disease Analysis, School of Public Health, Imperial College London, Faculty of Medicine, Nor